WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.100 --> 00:00:02.168
-Can we talk about
the coronavirus?

00:00:02.168 --> 00:00:03.703
-Yeah.
Are you stressed out about it?

00:00:03.703 --> 00:00:05.538
How are you feeling about it?
-I'm a little stressed.

00:00:05.538 --> 00:00:07.674
I'm trying not to be stressed
out, but I keep --

00:00:07.674 --> 00:00:09.709
everything I read and see
is scaring me.

00:00:09.709 --> 00:00:11.777
-Yeah.
-So I'm trying to be like,

00:00:11.777 --> 00:00:13.913
"Wait.
I want to take it seriously,

00:00:13.913 --> 00:00:15.948
but I want to know what --"
What is going on?

00:00:15.948 --> 00:00:18.184
Because I feel like I'm getting
mixed messages from

00:00:18.184 --> 00:00:21.120
the Trump administration
and what I'm seeing online

00:00:21.120 --> 00:00:22.988
and in newspapers.
-Yeah.

00:00:22.988 --> 00:00:26.825
I mean, it's a real thing.
It is worth taking seriously.

00:00:26.825 --> 00:00:29.862
And we're starting to see it
around the country now.

00:00:29.862 --> 00:00:32.698
It's in multiple states.
We've had multiple deaths.

00:00:32.698 --> 00:00:35.668
New York City has its first
confirmed case, which, you know,

00:00:35.668 --> 00:00:38.404
matters to those of us here
in New York in particular.

00:00:38.404 --> 00:00:42.775
But this is something that --
I mean, it is going to --

00:00:42.775 --> 00:00:44.844
it's spread in dozens of
countries around the world.

00:00:44.844 --> 00:00:47.580
It's likely to spread
around the entire world.

00:00:47.580 --> 00:00:49.182
A lot of people
are going to get it,

00:00:49.182 --> 00:00:51.984
potentially most of the world's
population are going to get it,

00:00:51.984 --> 00:00:55.788
which sounds terrible,
but most of the people

00:00:55.788 --> 00:00:58.891
who get coronavirus
will not die from it.

00:00:58.891 --> 00:01:00.226
-Okay.
-It's got about

00:01:00.226 --> 00:01:04.697
a 2% fatality rate.
And the way that we all

00:01:04.697 --> 00:01:07.500
need to deal with it,
although it is a new threat --

00:01:07.500 --> 00:01:09.235
we've never seen
this virus before --

00:01:09.235 --> 00:01:12.037
it's actually very old-fashioned
ways of protecting ourselves.

00:01:12.037 --> 00:01:14.206
You do all the things that
you do to protect yourself

00:01:14.206 --> 00:01:16.475
from getting a cold
or from getting the flu.

00:01:16.475 --> 00:01:18.844
We don't have to do
anything outrageous.

00:01:18.844 --> 00:01:20.846
We don't need to change
our lives drastically

00:01:20.846 --> 00:01:23.450
at an individual level.
You just need to be

00:01:23.450 --> 00:01:25.518
more vigilant
about washing your hands,

00:01:25.518 --> 00:01:27.387
make sure you don't
develop patterns

00:01:27.387 --> 00:01:28.822
where you don't touch your face.

00:01:28.822 --> 00:01:31.457
That's the way we tend to give
ourselves colds and the flu.

00:01:31.457 --> 00:01:34.060
So at an individual level,
there's no reason to panic,

00:01:34.060 --> 00:01:35.961
but it's a serious thing.
-I saw somewhere that said,

00:01:35.961 --> 00:01:37.630
"Don't sneeze
or cough on people."

00:01:37.630 --> 00:01:40.633
I go, "Were we doing that?"
[ Laughter ]

00:01:40.633 --> 00:01:43.236
"Were people doing that?"
-New Year's resolution.

00:01:43.236 --> 00:01:44.971
-It's like, "I gotta stop
coughing on people."

00:01:44.971 --> 00:01:47.706
People not getting
invited back to parties.

00:01:47.706 --> 00:01:50.443
I've seen a lot of the masks
around the city.

00:01:50.443 --> 00:01:54.013
Is that a --
-That's probably not a thing.

00:01:54.013 --> 00:01:56.516
I mean, you're seeing it, but
it's probably not that smart.

00:01:56.516 --> 00:01:58.651
The Surgeon General actually put
out a statement which was like,

00:01:58.651 --> 00:02:01.086
"You guys, stop buying masks!"
-Yeah, I saw that.

00:02:01.086 --> 00:02:04.991
-If you are sick, if you have
respiratory symptoms, that's --

00:02:04.991 --> 00:02:06.892
people are wearing a mask
in that circumstance

00:02:06.892 --> 00:02:09.262
because you're trying to avoid
giving it to other people.

00:02:09.262 --> 00:02:11.698
But in terms of being a healthy
person and trying to avoid

00:02:11.698 --> 00:02:14.933
getting infected, that's
probably not that rational.

00:02:14.933 --> 00:02:17.036
-And is Purell still --
-Sure.

00:02:17.036 --> 00:02:18.571
If you can't wash --
If you can't --

00:02:18.571 --> 00:02:20.740
First of all, get good
at washing your hands.

00:02:20.740 --> 00:02:22.709
-I'm fantastic at it.
-Me too.

00:02:22.709 --> 00:02:24.677
I've always been kind of
a hand hygiene person.

00:02:24.677 --> 00:02:26.346
-Me too. I love it.
-This is why we get along!

00:02:26.346 --> 00:02:28.013
-I know, exactly.
[ Laughter ]

00:02:28.013 --> 00:02:30.183
I always knew you were.
-20 seconds in between

00:02:30.183 --> 00:02:33.085
the hands, under the nails,
backs of the hands. Right?

00:02:33.085 --> 00:02:35.321
But you if can't do that,
if you can't get access

00:02:35.321 --> 00:02:37.389
to wash your hands,
then using a hand sanitizer

00:02:37.389 --> 00:02:40.927
that's got an adequate level of
alcohol in it is a good move.

00:02:40.927 --> 00:02:42.227
-Thank you, bud.

00:02:42.227 --> 00:02:44.096
I gotta say congratulations
again on the book.

00:02:44.096 --> 00:02:45.531
-Thank you very much.
-"Blowout."

00:02:45.531 --> 00:02:48.033
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:02:48.033 --> 00:02:49.568
Number one "New York Times"
best-seller.

00:02:49.568 --> 00:02:51.403
Not too shabby.
-Not too shabby.

00:02:51.403 --> 00:02:52.672
Thank you very much.

00:02:52.672 --> 00:02:54.006
-Now it's going international
these days, right?

00:02:54.006 --> 00:02:55.074
-It is.
It's being published

00:02:55.074 --> 00:02:56.509
in a few other countries,
which is cool.

00:02:56.509 --> 00:02:58.511
I get to see it,
like, in other languages.

00:02:58.511 --> 00:03:00.380
-Isn't that the best?
-Yeah, the British version of it

00:03:00.380 --> 00:03:02.215
is, like, thicker.
It makes it look longer

00:03:02.215 --> 00:03:03.516
because they put it
on fatter paper.

00:03:03.516 --> 00:03:04.584
-Wow.
-I was like, "Thanks!

00:03:04.584 --> 00:03:05.985
You made me look like
I did more work!"

00:03:05.985 --> 00:03:07.286
[ Laughter ]
Just saying.

00:03:07.286 --> 00:03:08.988
-We always love you.
Thank you so much for coming on.

00:03:08.988 --> 00:03:10.089
-Nice to see you, my friend.
-You're the best.

00:03:10.089 --> 00:03:11.357
Rachel Maddow.
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:03:11.357 --> 00:03:13.325
Check out her best-selling book,
"Blowout,"

00:03:13.325 --> 00:03:17.730
and "The Rachel Maddow Show"
at 9:00 p.m. on MSNBC.

